WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.201 --> 00:00:18.224
During the day of January 29, the island of Ceylon disappeared from the horizon.

2
00:00:19.225 --> 00:00:24.468
I consulted the map in the gallery and found that we had traveled 7,500 leagues.

3
00:00:25.469 --> 00:00:29.711
When we rose to the surface the next day, there was no land in sight, and we were

4
00:00:29.771 --> 00:00:31.212
once again alone on the ocean.

5
00:00:32.384 --> 00:00:33.625
"Where do you think we're headed now?"

6
00:00:34.065 --> 00:00:36.486
asked Ned, who had joined me on the platform.

7
00:00:36.506 --> 00:00:40.768
"We're going wherever our captain's fancy takes us," I replied.

8
00:00:40.788 --> 00:00:45.670
"When I looked at our course on the map, we seemed headed toward the Persian Gulf."

9
00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:49.771
"Captain Nemo's fancy won't take us very far," said Ned.

10
00:00:49.791 --> 00:00:52.112
"The Persian Gulf has no outlet.

11
00:00:52.513 --> 00:00:55.994
So if we do enter the Gulf, it won't be long before we have to turn around!"

12
00:00:56.014 --> 00:01:00.296
"Very well, then we'll come out again and maybe we'll visit the Red Sea."

13
00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:02.581
"The Red Sea!"

14
00:01:03.041 --> 00:01:03.662
scoffed Ned.

15
00:01:03.682 --> 00:01:05.263
"It's a dead end too.

16
00:01:05.663 --> 00:01:10.988
The Suez Canal isn't finished, and even if it were, the captain wouldn't risk taking his submarine through

17
00:01:11.008 --> 00:01:11.868
the canal's locks.

18
00:01:12.369 --> 00:01:15.651
No, the Red Sea is not the route that will take us back to Europe."

19
00:01:16.812 --> 00:01:21.216
"Perhaps we will reenter the Indian Ocean and travel down the east coast of Africa until we reach

20
00:01:21.236 --> 00:01:22.097
the Cape of Good Hope."

21
00:01:23.138 --> 00:01:24.759
"And then where will we go next?"

22
00:01:25.179 --> 00:01:25.680
Ned asked.

23
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:30.684
"Well, then we'll enter a section of the Atlantic Ocean that we're not yet familiar with."

24
00:01:31.712 --> 00:01:35.815
I could tell that Ned was getting tired of this journey under the sea, but I was still

25
00:01:35.855 --> 00:01:37.215
an enthusiastic traveler.

26
00:01:37.235 --> 00:01:42.578
"For my sake, I'll be sorry to see the end of a voyage that so few men have

27
00:01:42.598 --> 00:01:43.879
had the opportunity to make."

28
00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:46.381
"Don't you realize, Professor?"

29
00:01:46.881 --> 00:01:47.321
asked Ned.

30
00:01:47.341 --> 00:01:51.143
"It will soon be three whole months that we've been imprisoned here?"

31
00:01:51.163 --> 00:01:56.246
"No, Ned, I didn't realize that; in fact, I don't want to realize it.

32
00:01:56.627 --> 00:01:58.508
I don't keep track of every day and every hour."

33
00:01:59.648 --> 00:02:02.369
"But when will our imprisonment come to an end?"

34
00:02:02.829 --> 00:02:03.529
Ned persisted.

35
00:02:03.549 --> 00:02:05.230
"When it ends.

36
00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:07.710
Meanwhile there's nothing we can do about it.

37
00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:12.051
If you tell me someday that we have a good opportunity to escape, then I'll listen to you.

38
00:02:12.812 --> 00:02:15.952
But until then, there's no point in even discussing this anymore."

39
00:02:15.972 --> 00:02:21.494
"Waiting and staying silent may be fine for you, but in my opinion, a life in prison is

40
00:02:21.534 --> 00:02:22.294
no life at all."

41
00:02:23.394 --> 00:02:26.095
And with that statement, Ned left me alone on the platform.

42
00:02:27.235 --> 00:02:31.561
It turned out that we were headed for the Red Sea, which we reached on February 7.

43
00:02:32.642 --> 00:02:36.845
I don't know why Captain Nemo decided to enter this body of water, but I approved of his

44
00:02:36.885 --> 00:02:39.606
decision because we began traveling at a slower speed.

45
00:02:40.987 --> 00:02:46.131
This gave me plenty of opportunities to observe life below the sea as well as the ancient towns

46
00:02:46.211 --> 00:02:47.111
along its shores.

47
00:02:48.212 --> 00:02:53.275
I spent hours at the gallery windows, gazing at species of plants and animals that I had never

48
00:02:53.355 --> 00:02:53.876
seen before.

49
00:02:54.952 --> 00:02:59.133
A couple days later, I was standing on the platform when Captain Nemo came up to take the

50
00:02:59.173 --> 00:02:59.914
ship's bearings.

51
00:02:59.934 --> 00:03:04.295
"Well, Professor Aronnax, does the Red Sea please you?

52
00:03:04.735 --> 00:03:10.997
Have you observed the wonders it holds: schools of fish, groupings of sponges, forests of coral?"

53
00:03:11.997 --> 00:03:15.898
"Yes, Captain, and the Nautilus is wonderful for conducting such a study.

54
00:03:16.518 --> 00:03:18.319
It is truly a marvelous boat!"

55
00:03:18.339 --> 00:03:21.300
"It is marvelous and indestructible.

56
00:03:21.728 --> 00:03:26.492
Traveling on this submarine, we do not need to fear the terrible storms of the Red Sea, or

57
00:03:26.532 --> 00:03:27.893
its currents and sandbanks."

58
00:03:29.315 --> 00:03:31.857
"Your boat is at least a century before its time."

59
00:03:32.257 --> 00:03:33.478
I continued with my praise.

60
00:03:33.498 --> 00:03:37.862
"How sad that the secret of this invention will die with its inventor."

61
00:03:38.843 --> 00:03:40.504
Captain Nemo was silent for a few minutes.

62
00:03:40.524 --> 00:03:44.888
"It's a pity I can't take you through the Suez Canal," he said at last.

63
00:03:44.908 --> 00:03:50.528
"But you'll be able to see the long jetty of Port Said the day after tomorrow, when we'll

64
00:03:50.548 --> 00:03:51.809
be in the Mediterranean Sea."

65
00:03:51.829 --> 00:03:55.211
"We'll be in the Mediterranean Sea the day after tomorrow?"

66
00:03:55.632 --> 00:03:56.192
I exclaimed.

67
00:03:57.233 --> 00:04:01.576
I was surprised, even though by now nothing about the Nautilus should have surprised me.

68
00:04:01.596 --> 00:04:06.219
"We'll have to travel at a rapid speed if we are to round the Cape of Good Hope

69
00:04:06.259 --> 00:04:07.700
and reach the Mediterranean by then!"

70
00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:12.523
"Who said that we would sail down the coast of Africa and around the Cape of Good Hope?"

71
00:04:12.923 --> 00:04:13.804
Captain Nemo replied.

72
00:04:13.824 --> 00:04:22.021
"Well, unless the Nautilus sails on dry land and passes above the isthmus—" "Or beneath it, Professor Aronnax,"

73
00:04:22.381 --> 00:04:23.041
he interrupted me.

74
00:04:23.741 --> 00:04:24.181
"Beneath it?"

75
00:04:24.621 --> 00:04:25.021
I echoed.

76
00:04:25.041 --> 00:04:27.942
"That's right," replied Captain Nemo.

77
00:04:27.962 --> 00:04:32.583
"There is a subterranean passage that I have named the Arabian Tunnel.

78
00:04:33.044 --> 00:04:36.264
It takes us beneath the Suez and opens into the Mediterranean.

79
00:04:36.684 --> 00:04:40.926
Not only does this passage exist, but I have made use of it several times.

80
00:04:41.206 --> 00:04:43.506
Without it, I wouldn't have ventured into the Red Sea."

81
00:04:44.567 --> 00:04:45.607
"But how did you find it?"

82
00:04:45.987 --> 00:04:46.407
I asked.

83
00:04:46.427 --> 00:04:50.184
"A combination of luck and reasoning," he replied.

84
00:04:51.225 --> 00:04:56.187
Captain Nemo then explained that he had noticed there were some identical species of fish in the Red

85
00:04:56.307 --> 00:04:57.787
Sea and the Mediterranean.

86
00:04:58.828 --> 00:05:03.870
He guessed that the subterranean current ran from the Red Sea to the Mediterranean because the Red Sea

87
00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:04.450
was higher.

88
00:05:05.911 --> 00:05:08.812
So he caught a large number of fish in the vicinity of Suez.

89
00:05:09.572 --> 00:05:13.474
He put a copper ring through their tails and threw them back into the Red Sea.

90
00:05:14.614 --> 00:05:18.156
A few months later he caught some of these fish in the eastern Mediterranean.

91
00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:22.022
"I then looked for the tunnel with the Nautilus," said the captain.

92
00:05:22.042 --> 00:05:26.705
"I passed through it, and the day after tomorrow, you will too."

93
00:05:27.806 --> 00:05:31.008
On the night of February 11, the Nautilus remained underwater.

94
00:05:31.989 --> 00:05:35.411
According to my calculations, we must have been very near Suez.

95
00:05:35.431 --> 00:05:40.835
"It can't be easy to pass through this tunnel of yours," I said to Captain Nemo as we

96
00:05:40.895 --> 00:05:42.296
sat in the gallery after supper.

97
00:05:42.316 --> 00:05:47.880
"No, and for that reason I usually go into the pilothouse and steer the submarine myself.

98
00:05:48.276 --> 00:05:49.216
You're welcome to come with me."

99
00:05:50.177 --> 00:05:52.797
We made our way to the pilothouse on top of the submarine.

100
00:05:54.178 --> 00:05:59.119
The cabin was dark, but soon I could see the pilot, a strong man who stood with his

101
00:05:59.179 --> 00:06:00.779
hands resting on the large wheel.

102
00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:06.521
Outside, the sea was brightly illuminated by the electric light behind the pilothouse.

103
00:06:06.541 --> 00:06:09.202
"Now," Captain Nemo said.

104
00:06:09.222 --> 00:06:11.322
"Let's look for our passageway."

105
00:06:12.342 --> 00:06:17.064
We traveled for almost an hour with Captain Nemo directing the pilot to make small changes in the

106
00:06:17.104 --> 00:06:17.964
submarine's course.

107
00:06:19.092 --> 00:06:24.494
"There!" exclaimed Nemo as a passage, black and deep, finally opened before us.

108
00:06:25.474 --> 00:06:29.755
The captain took the wheel and I heard the waters of the Red Sea roaring through the tunnel.

109
00:06:30.775 --> 00:06:35.557
On the walls of the narrow passage, I could see nothing but bright streaks from our electric light.

110
00:06:36.597 --> 00:06:41.758
Our engines churned the waves and the Nautilus went with the torrent, as swift as an arrow.

111
00:06:42.518 --> 00:06:44.159
My heart pounded furiously.

112
00:06:45.279 --> 00:06:49.182
At last Captain Nemo handed the wheel back to the pilot and turned to me.

113
00:06:49.202 --> 00:06:51.106
"The Mediterranean!"

114
00:06:51.687 --> 00:06:52.950
he announced triumphantly.
